# Budget Tracker — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# The Go server loads `.env` automatically on startup; the Python report script
# reads it too.

# ---------- Database (PostgreSQL / Supabase) ----------
# Paste the connection string from Supabase:
#   Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string -> URI
#
# Use the "Session pooler" (or "Direct connection") string on port 5432.
# Avoid the "Transaction pooler" on port 6543 — it does not work well with the
# prepared statements a long-running server keeps open.
#
# If your password contains @ : / or ?, percent-encode it, or simply copy the
# full URI exactly as the dashboard shows it.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.[YOUR-PROJECT-REF]:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-0-[YOUR-REGION].pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Optional: set at least 32 characters or the server refuses to start.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Changing this value invalidates everyone's login sessions.
SESSION_KEY=

# Optional: port the web server listens on. Defaults to 4000.
# Render sets this automatically.
PORT=4000

# Optional: the public URL the app is reachable at once deployed, with no
# trailing slash — e.g. https://budget-tracker.onrender.com
# Used to build correct password-reset links behind a proxy, and to decide
# whether session cookies get the Secure flag. On Render both are detected
# automatically (from RENDER / RENDER_EXTERNAL_URL), so this only matters for
# a custom domain or another host.
# APP_BASE_URL=

# Optional: force the session-cookie Secure flag on or off. Only needed to
# override the automatic detection described above. Leave unset for local
# plain-HTTP development, or logins will appear to do nothing.
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# ---------- Alternative to DATABASE_URL (local Postgres / manual config) ----------
# Only used when DATABASE_URL is empty or still a placeholder.
# DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=postgres
# DB_PASSWORD=
# DB_NAME=budget
# DB_SSLMODE=disable   # defaults to "disable" for localhost, "require" elsewhere

# ---------- Password-reset emails (Resend) ----------
# Without these, the forgot-password flow logs the reset link to the console
# instead of sending mail.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=onboarding@resend.dev

# ---------- Bot protection (Google reCAPTCHA v2) ----------
# Guards the signup form: a new account also needs a successfully solved
# "I'm not a robot" challenge.
#
# Create a free v2 checkbox key pair here:
#     https://www.google.com/recaptcha/admin/create
# Then add every domain the app is served from (e.g. localhost and
# your-app.onrender.com) to that key's allowed-domains list — the widget
# refuses to render on a domain that is not listed.
#
# RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY is public and is rendered into the signup page.
# RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY is a server-side secret: never commit it, never let it
# reach the browser. It is read only in the siteverify call in main.go.
#
# Leaving both unset is fine for local development: the widget is omitted and
# the check is skipped (with a logged warning). On a deployed HTTPS instance a
# missing secret makes signup REFUSE rather than silently let bots through —
# see recaptchaRequired() in main.go for why that direction was chosen.
# RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
# RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY=
